; Nightjar — KWin rule: paint Thunderbird's window decoration in the theme's ink.
;
; Thunderbird draws its own titlebar on the main window and on no other, so every
; secondary window — compose, dialogs, the key manager — gets one from the window
; manager instead, in the desktop's color. A user stylesheet cannot reach a window
; decoration. This closes it from the other side. See docs/kde.md and
; .agents/decisions/011-the-window-decoration-is-not-ours.md.
;
; Install:
;   cp contrib/kde/Nightjar.colors ~/.local/share/color-schemes/
;   System Settings → Window Management → Window Rules → Import…  (this file)
;   qdbus6 org.kde.KWin /KWin reconfigure
;
; Import is the safe route: it merges with rules you already have. Appending this
; file to ~/.config/kwinrulesrc by hand does not, because the group name and the
; [General] index would collide with an existing rule.
;
; Two things this file encodes that are easy to get wrong, both of them measured
; rather than assumed:
;
;   * The window class is org.mozilla.Thunderbird, not thunderbird. On Wayland
;     KWin reads the app id from the desktop file, and its substring matching is
;     case-sensitive, so a rule written for the lowercase name matches nothing —
;     silently, which looks exactly like a rule that matched and did nothing.
;   * There is no title condition, so this covers every Thunderbird window. On the
;     main window it is a no-op: Thunderbird draws that titlebar itself and the
;     window manager has nothing to recolor. To narrow it to the compose window
;     only, add title=Write: with titlematch=2 — bearing in mind captions are
;     locale-dependent.
;
; decocolorrule=2 is Force. wmclassmatch=1 is Exact.

[e5f4a1c2-9d3b-4c7e-8a16-nightjar0001]
Description=Nightjar — Thunderbird window decoration
decocolor=Nightjar
decocolorrule=2
wmclass=org.mozilla.Thunderbird
wmclasscomplete=false
wmclassmatch=1
